# Busting 6 Common Myths About Tax Resolution

When it comes to tax debt, misinformation spreads fast. A friend's horror story, a radio ad promising pennies on the dollar, or a scary-looking notice can leave you with the wrong idea about what's possible. Let's clear up some of the most common myths.

## Myth 1: "I have to deal with the IRS myself."

Not true. You have the right to representation. By signing a power of attorney, you can authorize a qualified tax professional to communicate with the IRS or your state tax agency for you. They can handle correspondence, request your records, and negotiate on your behalf, so you're not stuck navigating the system alone or guessing at the right thing to say.

## Myth 2: "Penalties are permanent."

Penalties can often be reduced or removed entirely. First-time penalty abatement is available to many taxpayers who've had a clean record for the past three years. If that doesn't apply, reasonable cause relief may be an option when something serious, like a major illness, a disaster, or a death in the family, got in the way of filing or paying on time.

## Myth 3: "Everyone can settle for pennies on the dollar."

This one gets advertised a lot, and it's misleading. An Offer in Compromise is a legitimate way to settle for less than you owe, but the IRS only accepts offers when your financial situation shows that full payment isn't realistic. Your income, expenses, assets, and future earning potential are all examined. Some people qualify; many don't. An honest evaluation before applying is essential.

## Myth 4: "If I can't pay in full, I'm out of luck."

Far from it. Installment agreements give you breathing room. A short-term payment plan allows up to 180 days to pay off your balance, which is perfect if you just need a little time. A long-term payment plan breaks the debt into monthly payments over a longer period, and many taxpayers can set one up online without much paperwork.

## Myth 5: "A payment plan means paying every last cent."

Not always. A partial payment installment agreement lets you make monthly payments you can actually afford, even if they won't cover the full balance before the IRS's collection period ends. That window is generally 10 years from when the tax was assessed, and any remaining balance after that point is typically no longer collectible. The IRS will review your finances carefully, but for the right person, this option can be a lifesaver.

## Myth 6: "Ignoring the problem will make it go away."

Unfortunately, the opposite is true. Penalties and interest keep growing, and unresolved balances can lead to liens, levies, and wage garnishments. The sooner you act, the more options you'll usually have.

